# `getOrElse(k, default)` on a receiver that is not a bare variable

`v2/BUGS.md` `v2-getorelse-two-arg-falls-into-option-helper`. Both self-hosted fronts define one
prelude helper `_sel_getOrElse` and it is `lam 2` — the OPTION shape `(opt, default)`. A Map's
`getOrElse` takes two arguments, so the call arrives with three and dies at run time:

    ssc: arity: 2 expected, 3 given

It survived because the legacy lowerer tracks map **variables** (`isMapVar`) and routes those to
`_sel_mapGetOrElse`. Track the receiver as a variable and it works; write the same call on a field,
on a literal, or on a call result and it does not.

THIS IS A KNOWN BUG CLASS, ALREADY FIXED ONCE FOR ITS SIBLING. `selMethodOr` carries the note that
`_sel_mkString` is arity-2, so the 0-arg and 3-arg forms crashed with this same message, and those
were routed to the runtime `__method__`. The remedy was applied to `mkString` and not to
`getOrElse`. `__method__` already has the arms — `(MapV, "getOrElse", List(k, d))` and the 1-arg
`Some`/`None` ones — so nothing new is needed at run time.

WHY NO CASE CAUGHT IT: of the 62 two-argument `getOrElse` call sites in this repository's `.ssc`
sources, 45 use the failing shape and 43 of those are in nine `examples/` files — while the
conformance corpus contained **none** until this case. The corpus exercised the one receiver shape
that happens to work.

The four receiver shapes are separate lines on purpose: only the first one passed before the fix, so
a case written with a bare variable would have been green in both states.

```scalascript
case class Box(m: Map[String, String])

def wrap(m: Map[String, String]) = m

def main() =
  val m = Map("a" -> "1")
  val b = Box(m)

  // bare variable — the only shape that worked before the fix
  println(m.getOrElse("a", "none"))
  println(m.getOrElse("zz", "none"))

  // field selector — the shape 43 example call sites use
  println(b.m.getOrElse("a", "none"))
  println(b.m.getOrElse("zz", "none"))

  // literal receiver
  println(Map("a" -> "1").getOrElse("a", "none"))
  println(Map("a" -> "1").getOrElse("zz", "none"))

  // call result
  println(wrap(m).getOrElse("a", "none"))
  println(wrap(m).getOrElse("zz", "none"))

  // the one-argument Option form must keep working — it is the same helper
  println(Some("x").getOrElse("none"))
  println(None.getOrElse("none"))
  val o: Option[String] = m.get("a")
  println(o.getOrElse("none"))
  println(m.get("zz").getOrElse("none"))
```

Expected — the JVM lane is the oracle:

    1
    none
    1
    none
    1
    none
    1
    none
    x
    none
    1
    none
